            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Exploration of Human Nature</strong><br>\nKubrick's films often explored the darker sides of human nature. \"A Clockwork Orange\" (1971) and \"The Shining\" (1980) delved into psychological horror, while \"Dr. Strangelove\" (1964) satirized the absurdity of political systems and the Cold War.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mastery of Various Genres</strong><br>\nKubrick was not tied to any one genre, mastering science fiction with \"2001: A Space Odyssey\" (1968), period drama with \"Barry Lyndon\" (1975), and erotic drama with \"Eyes Wide Shut\" (1999). His versatility showcased his ability to imprint his distinct vision across different filmic narratives.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Technological Innovations in Cinema</strong><br>\nAlways pushing the boundaries, Kubrick was known for his technological innovations, such as front projection in \"2001: A Space Odyssey\" and using specially adapted lenses to film scenes by candlelight in \"Barry Lyndon.\"</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
